  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The Dodgers took this one early. Michael King lasted four innings and gave up five runs in the second, and by then the game was already decided. The lineup managed just five hits and ten strikeouts. The kind of night that doesn't come back. They trailed after the second and never recovered.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what happens when pitching breaks and the bats stay silent. King couldn't locate, the defense couldn't slow it down, and an offense that's been clicking suddenly looked lost at the plate. Campusano went 2-for-4, which felt like showing up to a fight unarmed. One solid effort in a sea of empty swings. The bullpen. Peralta and Canning combining for four innings and holding the damage to two more runs. Did what it could with nothing to work with, but four outs don't change a game decided in one inning. This team has won eight of its last ten because both sides are working. Tonight, only one side showed up. You don't lose games like this on accident.",
    "what_to_watch": "Yoshinobu Yamamoto's ERA sits at 2.51, and Ray takes the ball tonight at 7:10 PT with the Padres looking to avoid another blowout against the Dodgers.",
